00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.240
Welcome back to "Laws Broken",

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.350
where an attorney destroys
your favorite movies

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.218
by showing you how illegal everything is

00:00:24.218 --> 00:00:27.150
because everything is illegal.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.990
Who doesn't love "Indiana Jones",

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.300
the story of a
swashbuckling hero professor

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.630
who saves the world from Nazi megalomania,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.580
and wins the heart of his fair maiden.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.327
Yes, I'm talking about
1981's box office smash,

00:00:38.327 --> 00:00:40.450
"Indiana Jones and the
Raiders of the Lost Ark".

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:41.370
You probably remember this

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.830
as wholesome family entertainment,

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.110
where a professor who happens
to be good with a whip

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.720
saves the world from the worst villains

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.960
on the planet, Nazis.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.070
The movie seems to have it all.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.880
Indiana Jones outwitting bad
guys and sporting a cool hat,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.350
an arrogant French batty
who gets his comeuppance

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.360
and a spirited heroine who falls in love

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.020
with the man who saves her life.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.900
Well, I hate to break it to you,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.740
but Indiana Jones isn't
exactly a humble archeologist.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.680
This is a movie where the
hero travels the world

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.910
looting graves and
temples, stealing artifacts

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.220
and permanently destroying
precious historical sites.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.020
You know, kind of like the Taliban.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.290
He admits to serious
crimes against the woman

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.640
that he's supposed to be in love with

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.580
and the movie also has an
absurdly high body count.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.560
And despite all of the
property destruction,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.360
grave robbing, murder,
kidnapping and rape,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.390
this movie inspired several
Disney park attractions,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.080
so come for a crazy ride with me

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.658
as we discover the dark
side of Indiana Jones

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:34.750
and all the laws broken.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.400
- Didn't you guys ever
go to Sunday School?

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.490
- So let's start with the grave robbing.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.010
If you thought it didn't seem right

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.420
that Indiana Jones was
stealing precious artifacts

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.210
and bringing them home
to the United States

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:47.630
and pillaging The Hovitos,
well, you might be right.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.910
Indiana Jones is an archeologist
who purports to save things

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.150
to put them in a museum,

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.480
but is he allowed to steal artifacts

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.080
from foreign countries and graves

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.710
to fill the Chicago Museum?

00:01:57.710 --> 00:01:58.543
No?

00:01:58.543 --> 00:01:59.376
Yes?

00:01:59.376 --> 00:02:00.230
Well, maybe.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.210
Jones may say he wants rare artifacts

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.340
to be placed in a museum,
but later in his apartment,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.500
we see a number of artifacts

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.700
that he probably didn't
buy from HomeGoods.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.580
So what's the real story on people

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.860
who find, buy, and sell artifacts?

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.910
Well, treasure hunters, antique dealers,

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:16.020
and even museums get busted all the time

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.200
for selling, or transferring

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.860
or transporting stolen artifacts.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.640
And now, for the part
you've been waiting for,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.950
is Indiana Jones a
grave robbing profiteer?

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.350
Well, antiques trading
can be perfectly legal

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.570
or totally illegal depending on where the

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.510
artifacts are found and how they are sold.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.390
As long as the person follows
the laws of the country of

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.390
origin regarding excavation, transport

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.470
and sale of archeological artifacts,

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.090
the trade is generally legal.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.700
However, there's also a
billion dollar black market

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.410
for ancient antique objects.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.190
There's no one international law that

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.390
all nations must follow.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.330
Instead, the artifacts
are protected by treaties

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.180
like the Hague Convention
for the Protection

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.680
of Cultural Property in the
Event of Armed Conflict.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.650
This is a treaty ratified by
132 countries that protects

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.950
cultural property during
wars and armed conflicts.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.950
There's also the Rome Statute establishing

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.340
the International Criminal Court,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.100
which makes it a war crime to deliberately

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.400
target religious, educational, artistic,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.060
or scientific properties for
destruction or defacement.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.670
The UNESCO Convention on
the Means of Prohibiting and

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.550
Preventing the Elicit
Import, Export and Transfer

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.280
of Ownership of Cultural
Property is meant to stop

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.990
the circulation of stolen items,

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.910
and this convention gives member nations

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.380
the right to recover stolen
or illegally exported

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.270
antiquities from member countries.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.190
Currently, 140 countries are
parties to this convention,

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.460
including Egypt, Nepal, Greece

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.240
and all of the South American countries

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.890
that Indian Jones may have visited.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.130
The United States ratified
the UNESCO Convention

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:36.060
and implemented it with the Convention

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.760
on Cultural Property
Implementation Act, or CIPA.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.930
CIPA applies if the objects are actually

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.834
cultural artifacts or they
are stolen or exported.

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:46.534
CIPA includes a safe harbor
provision that allows for

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:49.640
the import of cultural
artifacts to the United States

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.126
if the importer can provide
an export certificate

00:03:52.126 --> 00:03:53.730
from the foreign country

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.620
and the safe harbor provision also applies

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.970
if the importer can produce
satisfactory evidence that the

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.390
material was either
exported prior to the date

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:02.490
the import restrictions were
published in the federal

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.340
register or exported from
the foreign country at least

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.650
10 years prior to the date of
entry into the United States.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:11.100
Now, I can hear the
internet comments already.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.540
There was no CIPA when
Indiana Jones museumed

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.810
the stuff from the South American temple,

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.040
but it's worth noting that
this movie is set in the 1930s,

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.960
which was kind of a big
news decade in case you were

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.250
wondering and haven't
opened a history book.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.140
The Nazis really did loot and plunder

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.100
priceless works of art,

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.810
antique objects and property
of people they murdered

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.260
and these events actually
were the impetus for the

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.310
international treaties that
would one day stop Indiana Jones

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.830
from taking home souvenirs
from his archeological digs,

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.190
or at least force him to produce a permit

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.830
or satisfactory evidence showing that

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:45.000
he came by the objects legally.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.050
Lawyers, always making
people do more paperwork.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:49.990
In America, there is another important law

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.000
in this particular case,

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.640
the National Stolen Property Act, or NSPA,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.450
which makes it a felony to
knowingly possess, conceal,

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:00.980
sell or dispose of any goods
of the value of $5,000 or more,

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.630
which have crossed a state or

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.670
US boundary after being stolen.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.450
The federal government is more likely

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.280
to use this law than CIPA
when someone makes a claim

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.080
that an American illegally
acquired foreign antiquities.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.330
And under the NSPA,

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.150
the word stolen takes
on a different meaning

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.670
than what we're used
to in general parlous.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.900
Generally, when something
is removed from a person

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.550
or a business or
institution, that's theft,

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:26.140
but under the NSPA, an object
doesn't have to be actually

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.870
taken from a person or business.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.600
Stolen can also mean removed
from an abandoned place,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.180
like a temple or a grave
or historical site,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.970
that nobody officially owns.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:37.940
So it's illegal under the
NSPA to take something

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.984
from a foreign country
without explicit permission

00:05:39.984 --> 00:05:41.850
from its current government,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.900
or taking something with
permission of a colonial

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.710
or occupying force rather than from the

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.620
country's current legitimate government.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.940
So, Indiana Jones could try
to defend his theft by arguing

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.210
that nobody actually
owned the golden idol.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.970
However, the NSPA also
applies when a government

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.150
simply declares an artifact stolen.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.280
This is usually enough in
situations where it seems like

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.240
no one actually owns the
artifact in question.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.610
And when Jones took the
golden idol away from a temple

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:09.570
in the deep jungle of South America,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.400
let's just say it was from Brazil,

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.160
it was only a crime if
Brazil had passed a law

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.010
or otherwise declared it a
state-owned cultural object.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.160
If a foreign government doesn't

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.480
make an ownership declaration,

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.930
then some American courts
reason that prosecuting people

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.810
in the US for illegally
exporting artifacts from

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.140
foreign nations would simply
amount to the United States

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.580
and forcing the other
nation's laws for them.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.140
Now, Indiana Jones isn't
the only one engaging

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.990
in some suspicious activity here.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.410
There's also the treachery of Belloq,

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.900
but stealing the idol from
Dr. Jones in the first place

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.140
brings up another question,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.270
could Belloq get in trouble for stealing

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.260
the golden idol from Indy?

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.180
- Dr. Jones.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.440
Again, we see there is
nothing you can possess

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.090
which I cannot take away.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.590
- Well, in most jurisdictions,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.360
if a person accepts possession
of goods or property

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.840
and knew that they were
stolen, this is a crime.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.460
We don't want people claiming
that they didn't know the

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:01.810
thing was stolen in the first place

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.210
and just getting away with
selling clearly stolen property.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:06.760
And for the sake of this exploration,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.140
let's assume that Belloq
sets up a shop in the US

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.650
with the idol that he took from Dr. Jones.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.750
In the United States,
receipt of stolen property

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.950
is a federal crime under 18 U.S.C 2315,

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.120
defined as knowingly
receiving, concealing,

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.820
or disposing of stolen property with

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.750
a value of at least $5,000
that also constitutes

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:24.810
interstate commerce.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.810
If Belloq intended to move
the idol across state lines,

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:29.680
he could be prosecuted
for receipt of possession

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:30.780
of stolen property.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.470
He'd also be subject to
prosecution under the NSPA.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:36.090
Of course, receiving and
selling stolen property

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.480
is usually a state law crime,

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.000
so Belloq would certainly be guilty of all

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.645
of the state law crimes as well.

00:07:41.645 --> 00:07:44.003
- I'm a shadowy reflection of you.

00:07:45.348 --> 00:07:48.260
Will take only a nudge
to make you like me,

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.270
to push you out of the light.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.600
- And anyone who received
the idol from Belloq

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:53.750
would also be guilty.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.650
As law students learn
from their property and

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:56.800
criminal law courses,

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.910
a thief can't pass good
title to stolen objects,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.580
and a stolen object retains
its stolen status forever

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.280
no matter when the thief
happens to have taken the object

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:08.320
or how many different people
held onto that object.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.640
This actually happens with
some frequency in the art world

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.870
and for all of you budding
art collectors out there,

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.470
an object status is
stolen also doesn't change

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.940
if someone buys it in good
faith down the line without

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.630
knowledge that it was
stolen many owners ago.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.280
Many disappointed art
collectors and museums have been

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.190
forced to return objects
that they thought they had

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:28.170
purchased from a reputable source.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:31.138
Hobby Lobby recently had
to return thousands of

00:08:31.138 --> 00:08:33.908
Middle Eastern artifacts because

00:08:33.908 --> 00:08:36.240
they had been wrongfully acquired.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.200
This is why Jones' ultimate
motive for removing the items

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.240
doesn't really matter.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.390
So, Jones went to South
American and entered the

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.750
sanctuary of a temple and
removed the golden idol.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.510
Did he have a permit for that expedition?

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.350
It doesn't look like it,

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.950
and that means he didn't have
the permission of whoever

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.150
owned the site or whatever
government is in power.

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:53.820
And although this particular expedition

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.100
probably isn't a war crime,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.980
it's still serious enough
that Jones is guilty

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.320
of violating the UNESCO
Convention as well as CIPA.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.130
He's also in big trouble for
removing objects from all over

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.230
the world and bringing
them back to the US,

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:05.930
even if some of the sites were abandoned,

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.950
like the sanctuary in
the opening sequence,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.160
probably doesn't matter and
Jones could expect to be

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:13.310
prosecuted as a repeat
offender under the NSPA.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.570
And although Jones seals
the Ark of the Covenant,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.110
it's unclear whether the US
government would prosecute him

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.360
for the theft under CIPA or the NSPA,

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.464
but I have been informed that
the US government has top men

00:09:23.464 --> 00:09:26.040
working on this investigation.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.923
- Top men?

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:29.840
- Top men.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.670
So, that's your general history lesson.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.240
Now, let's move on to
interpersonal communications.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.530
Generally in American cinema,

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.820
we don't like our protagonist engaging

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.750
in questionable sexual behavior.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:40.900
We're not the French,

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:42.720
but here's a scene you
probably don't remember

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.960
seeing the first time around.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.000
Recall that Jones has an
affair with Marion Ravenwood,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.270
his professor's daughter.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.563
Seems normal.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:52.830
- I learned to hate you
in the last 10 years.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:56.150
- I never meant to hurt you.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.260
- I was a child!

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.840
I was in love!

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.330
It was wrong, and you knew it!

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:01.480
- Yikes.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.410
- I did what I did.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:03.710
You don't have to be happy about it,

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.690
but maybe we could help
each other out now.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.700
- Double yikes.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:10.000
Did Indiana Jones just admit
to the fact of statutory rape,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.010
but then say he was tired of saying sorry,

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.680
then he needed a $3,000 gold medallion?

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.230
Well, here's the context,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:18.900
and I'm not sure that that
actually makes it any better.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.580
The "Raiders of the Lost Ark" screenplay

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.110
was pretty clear about Marion's age.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.790
It says she was about 25 years
old when the movie begins.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.930
Marion was the daughter of
Jones' mentor, Abner Ravenwood.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.710
Jones said he hadn't spoken
to Marion for 10 years,

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.610
which means that 10 years
ago, they had a relationship.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:34.470
Here's the problem.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.364
If Marion was 25 during the film,

00:10:36.364 --> 00:10:39.250
then she was with Jones 10 years earlier

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:40.900
when she was just 15 years old.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.820
And since that description is offscreen,

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.420
we could maybe guesstimate that Lucas,

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:47.150
Spielberg and screenwriter
Lawrence Kasdan were not thinking

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:48.870
that precisely about Marion's age

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:50.910
and hadn't gone to all the trouble

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:54.430
of subtracting 10 from 25 and getting 15,

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.450
but we would probably be wrong.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.283
A few years ago,

00:10:57.283 --> 00:10:59.020
a transcript of a
discussion between Lucas,

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.800
Spielberg and Kasdan made the rounds.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.430
The three men talked about Marion's age

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:03.750
and her involvement with Jones.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.550
They decided that she had
to be under the age of 16

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:08.470
when she was with Jones, so yes,

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:09.890
they knew what they were doing.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.850
And onscreen, Marion says she was a child

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.920
and Jones doesn't deny that it happened.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:17.900
He even tells her that
she needs to get over it.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:18.840
- I did what I did.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:20.187
You don't have to be happy about it,

00:11:20.187 --> 00:11:22.087
but maybe we can help each other out now.

00:11:22.087 --> 00:11:23.570
- #MeToo.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:25.510
Now, this is not a laughing matter.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.130
Statutory rape is a crime that
involves sexual contact with

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.350
a person who is under
the age specified by law,

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.370
commonly referred to
as the age of consent.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.480
The age of consent differs
from state to state

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.350
and so does the name of the crime.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.430
And most states no longer
refer to this crime

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:39.970
as statutory rape, instead,

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:42.090
using terms such as sexual
intercourse with a minor,

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:43.540
sexual assault of a child,

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.870
or criminal sexual
penetration of a minor child

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:46.910
under a certain age.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:49.340
Now, statutory rape in general
is based on the notion that

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.410
a person under a certain
age cannot consent

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.920
to sexual contact or activity because

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:55.650
he or she lacks the maturity or judgment

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:59.150
necessary to make a knowing
choice about sexual activity,

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.070
and statutory rape is one of the rare,

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.240
strict liability criminal
offenses out there.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.010
That means that the mental
state of the offender

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:07.250
isn't really relevant.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:08.916
If the sexual contact happened,

00:12:08.916 --> 00:12:11.800
the defendant has no defense
because the victim is deemed

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:14.210
too young to have been
capable of consenting.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.900
That means that even if
the defendant honestly

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.180
and reasonably believed that
the minor was old enough

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.550
to give legal consent, they are
still guilty of the offense.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.860
And that's not even the
situation we have in this film.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.460
Here, Indiana Jones was
mentored by Marion's father

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.150
and he knew her when she was growing up.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:30.083
He agrees that yes,

00:12:30.083 --> 00:12:31.904
he had a relationship with her when

00:12:31.904 --> 00:12:34.110
she was a minor and knew it.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:35.720
- I never meant to hurt you.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:37.260
- It was wrong, and you knew it!

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.820
- And for jurisdictional purposes,

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.010
let's assume that this
happened in Illinois,

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.160
where Jones was living

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:43.970
when he was at the University of Chicago.

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:46.480
If Marion was 15 at the
time she was with Indy,

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:47.830
then he would be in big trouble

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:49.800
regardless of whether she was in love

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.380
or he thought she could consent.

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.270
And Jones could also be prosecuted for

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.190
aggravated criminal sexual abuse because

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:56.380
he was in a position of trust,

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.620
authority or supervision over Marion.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:02.080
Remember, he was a teaching
assistant to her father and may

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:04.610
be considered someone who
was in a position of trust.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:07.750
So the conclusion is that Jones
is guilty of second degree

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:10.300
sexual assault because
she was 15 years old

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:12.220
when he had a relationship with her

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.770
and he was more than five
years older than she was.

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:17.990
Indiana Jones doesn't
really have a defense

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:19.830
and can expect a minimum jail term

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:21.700
of three to seven years in prison,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.350
a fine of up to $25,000, or both.

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:26.850
He'd also have to register
as a sex offender.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.110
So, how are we feeling
about the good old days

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.343
of family friendly entertainment now?

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:33.323
Yikes.

00:13:33.323 --> 00:13:35.700
So, let's move on to
something that is a little bit

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.860
more fun and lighthearted.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:38.693
Murder!

00:13:38.693 --> 00:13:41.250
You may recall that Indiana
Jones is being chased through

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.400
the streets of Cairo by a bunch of goons

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:44.510
when he hits a dead end and there,

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:46.140
he finds a man with a sword.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.450
After the man impressively
demonstrates how he would

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:49.330
cut Jones up,

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:52.020
Indiana Jones pulls out a
gun and shoots the man dead.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.690
This scene was not brought
to you by Smith and Wesson.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.040
The original scene was supposed
to be an epic sword fight

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.252
that would take three days
to film, but at the time,

00:13:59.252 --> 00:14:01.900
Ford kept running into a stomach bug.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:02.930
He asked Steven Spielberg

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.240
if he could just, quote,
"Shoot the son of a (bleep)."

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:08.081
Indiana Jones, brought
to you by dysentery.

00:14:08.081 --> 00:14:09.740
(drum music)
(gunshot)

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:11.870
But what about his foe in this movie?

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.490
Was Jones justified in shooting this man?

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:18.430
Was it murder or could he
shoot this SOB in self-defense?

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:20.340
Well, American law generally
has different rules

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:22.580
for the use of deadly
force in self-defense

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:25.610
than use of nondeadly
force in self-defense.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:28.100
And generally, you can use
nondeadly force to defend

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:31.590
yourself against any unlawful
attack and also to defend your

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:33.980
property against theft or unlawful damage.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:36.110
Now, it varies slightly state by state,

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:39.130
but when you're defending
yourself from nondeadly force,

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.150
you can only use the
amount of force necessary

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:42.940
to fend off the attacker,

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.470
and you should also probably
retreat, if possible.

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:47.400
Now, traditionally, you
could use deadly force

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:49.570
to defend yourself only if you were being

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.840
threatened with death,
serious bodily injury,

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.010
rape or kidnapping.

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.940
So, imagine that someone
takes a swing at you

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:55.800
outside of a bar.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.520
They miss, but they're
mad and you know it.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:14:59.400
What can you do?

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.470
You can meet their violence
only with the amount of force

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.440
necessary to fend off the attacker.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.100
Therefore, you can punch the person

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.330
to prevent them from hitting you,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.833
but you can't pull out a
gun and shoot the puncher

00:15:08.833 --> 00:15:10.500
because in this hypothetical,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.450
you didn't reasonably fear for your life

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:14.130
just because someone wanted to fight you.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:17.360
You could also leave the
scene and flee entirely.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.000
However, things get more complicated

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.580
when the person threatening
you is brandishing objects that

00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:23.950
could inflict more damage than a fist.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:26.320
Can you shoot someone
for brandishing a sword?

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.520
During the 1930s when
this movie took place,

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.180
if you were outside of your home and were

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:33.100
approached by an aggressor,
you could only use deadly force

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.330
if someone attacked you with deadly force.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.427
A famous treatise on criminal
law states that quote,

00:15:37.427 --> 00:15:39.970
"Merely to threaten death
or serious bodily harm,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.955
without any intention
to carry out the threat,

00:15:41.955 --> 00:15:44.330
is not to use deadly force,

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:47.640
so that one may be justified
in pointing a gun at his

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.400
attacker when he would not be

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.000
justified pulling the trigger."

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.010
Following these principles,

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:54.980
a guy could brandish that
sword without triggering Jones'

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.400
right to use deadly force,

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:59.100
and many state courts have
held that a defendant's mere

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.500
display of a knife during a
fight while implying the threat

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.460
of violence does not
constitute deadly force.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:07.960
The prosecution would argue
that Jones just stood there,

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:10.140
which shows that there
was plenty of time for him

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:11.810
to run or even walk away,

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:14.530
but Indiana Jones' lawyers
would obviously say that

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:16.840
there was nowhere for him to
run since he had already been

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:18.600
attacked by people in the market and was

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:19.970
surrounded by bad guys.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.940
In modern law, there's a trend
to give people greater rights

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.790
to use deadly force because America.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:29.110
But many state laws say that a
person must have a reasonable

00:16:29.110 --> 00:16:31.262
belief of imminent death or injury

00:16:31.262 --> 00:16:34.400
and the inability to retreat in safety

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.170
in order to use lethal force.

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:38.550
And then there are the so-called
stand your ground laws,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.210
which give people the right to
use deadly force in any place

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.240
where they have a legal right to be

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.220
as long as they reasonably feared

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:46.900
serious bodily harm or death,

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:48.900
they don't have the duty to retreat.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.120
US states are pretty evenly split between

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.970
stand your ground laws
and duty to retreat laws.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.780
Now, it's possible that Indiana
Jones would have a good case

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:58.890
for self-defense under
these modern, flexible,

00:16:58.890 --> 00:16:59.870
deadly force laws,

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.290
but the biggest problem
is that Jones doesn't even

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:03.440
try to get away.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.080
Indiana Jones would have to
prove that he's in distress,

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:08.150
in fear of his life and he has no choice

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.880
but to shoot his way out of a jam.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:13.130
However, that's a hard argument
to make when he's literally

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:14.980
just standing there
looking pretty relaxed,

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:15.960
given the situation.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:18.420
And there's also a lot of
witnesses who don't exactly look

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:20.430
like they'd be favorable to his defense.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.400
So, the conclusion here is
that Jones is probably guilty

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:24.990
of some variety of murder here.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:27.250
Since this wasn't a premeditated crime,

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:28.970
he could be convicted
of second degree murder,

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:31.170
which is typically defined
as a murder that is not

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:34.280
premeditated or murder that
is caused by the offender's

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.070
reckless conduct that displays an obvious

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:37.600
lack of concern for human life.

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:40.220
The penalty for second degree
murder in most jurisdictions

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:41.800
is about 10 years to life.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:45.150
Now, before we tally up all the
crimes against Indiana Jones

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:46.684
and see how long he's going to jail,

00:17:46.684 --> 00:17:49.150
let's talk about the dishonorable mentions

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:50.978
of all of the other crimes
that were committed.

00:17:50.978 --> 00:17:53.330
(amusing trumpet music)

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.040
Indiana Jones is not the only one

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:56.610
who's up to no good in this movie,

00:17:56.610 --> 00:18:00.190
and the cartoonish violence
includes a lot of carnage.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.430
There are of course lots
of bad guy crimes by people

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:03.660
who work for the Nazis.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.380
In Nepal, they burned down
Marion's bar during a gunfight

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:07.213
that they started.

00:18:07.213 --> 00:18:08.046
This is arson.

00:18:08.046 --> 00:18:10.190
They're also guilty of
murder and attempted murder

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.460
many times over.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.410
The baddies did a lot
of criming during the

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:14.550
market scene in Egypt.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.790
Look at all the unprovoked
battery offenses

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.540
with punches and various
weapons, and again,

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:20.400
attempted murders.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.571
Here, they're kidnapping
Marion inside of a basket.

00:18:22.571 --> 00:18:24.005
- [Marion] You can't do this to me!

00:18:24.005 --> 00:18:25.600
I'm an American!

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:27.580
- And here, Indy avoids
just being stabbed.

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:30.040
This wasn't attempted murder
since the guy had a long knife

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:31.640
and wanted to kill Dr. Jones.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.410
Under US law, when Indy
moves away at the last second

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:37.000
and the guy stabbed someone
else to death by accident,

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:39.020
he can still be charged
with felony murder.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:40.960
Indiana Jones pushing
and shoving people and

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:43.340
opening their baskets
forcefully constitutes

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:45.360
assault and battery and
trespass to chattels.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:46.193
And of course,

00:18:46.193 --> 00:18:48.660
there's no exception because
he's looking for Marion.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.650
Some random beggars robbed
Indiana Jones while he was hiding

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:52.483
around the corner.

00:18:52.483 --> 00:18:55.130
Indiana Jones shoots up a
vehicle he thinks Marion is in.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.110
It catches fire and everybody dies.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:57.943
Whoopsie.

00:18:57.943 --> 00:18:58.776
- Whoopsie.

00:18:58.776 --> 00:19:01.630
- Bare chested Nazi commits
some very poorly choreographed

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:04.650
batteries against Indiana
Jones and then dies

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:06.460
in a way that might be negligent homicide.

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:07.340
While driving a truck,

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:09.740
Indiana Jones takes out some
Bedouins building a house

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:10.650
on the side of the road.

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.430
At the very least, negligent homicide.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:13.460
During the chase scene,

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:15.640
Jones has to throw a
lot of guys off a truck.

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.440
Let's declare this
self-defense, so take one away.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.930
A German U-boat intercepts
Indiana Jones' boat

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:23.000
to steal back the ark and kidnap Marion.

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:23.833
Now, luckily for you,

00:19:23.833 --> 00:19:26.600
I'm not going to go off the
deep end about maritime law,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:28.780
but boarding a foreign
flagged vessel constitutes

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:31.110
interference with the
normal freedom of navigation

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.200
and lawful passage in accordance
with the law of the sea.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:35.850
And finally, this monkey sells
out Marion to the bad guys,

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.784
clearly committing a
conspiracy to kidnap Marion,

00:19:38.784 --> 00:19:41.768
but monkeys can't be convicted of crimes.

00:19:41.768 --> 00:19:44.600
Then of course, there's
Belloq who gets his hands

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:47.277
very dirty, who is but
a shadowy reflection of

00:19:47.277 --> 00:19:50.590
Indiana Jones who commits
multiple crimes against Marion,

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:52.480
including kidnapping, false imprisonment,

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.950
assault, battery and
threatened with attempted rape.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:55.870
For statutory rape,

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.670
he's probably looking at at
least three years in prison.

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.470
Belloq locks Jones in a tomb.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.790
This is both false
imprisonment and kidnapping.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.860
And Belloq tries to kill Jones at least

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:06.450
10 times in this movie,

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.950
which is 10 counts of
either attempted murder

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.800
or conspiracy to commit murder.

